repl_rebase — implementation plan
==================================

status: done (2026-07-09)

references I1..I5 (plans/repl_list); pin lifecycle owned by
plans/repl_pin. pass-through ("rebase" => keys ["repl"]).

advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          repl_rebase
description   "Re-resolve a REPL's init specs against the CURRENT
              pin versions and rebuild its base theory. All steps are
              marked stale — call repl_replay afterwards to
              re-execute them on the new base. Only works on REPLs
              created by repl_init from theory/pin specs; fails if
              any referenced pin is stale (re-pin it first). A REPL
              already on the latest pins replies 'already up to
              date'."
              (source: mcp_server.py rebase :407 + ir.ML:587.)
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {"repl": {"type": "string"}},
               "required": ["repl"]}
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": false, "idempotentHint": true,
               "openWorldHint": false}
              (idempotent in effect: a second rebase is "already up
              to date".)
outputSchema  none.

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call "repl_rebase", {"repl": "B"}
  scala builtin row backend.ir("rebase", [(repl, B)])
  ml dispatcher     "rebase" -> Ir.rebase (get "repl")
  engine            ir.ML:587 — From_Theory origins only; resolves
                    specs, rebuilds base via Theory.begin_theory,
                    mark_all_stale; out "Rebased \"B\" (N steps
                    marked stale; use replay to re-execute)"

implementation steps
--------------------

1. [this tool] builtin row + handler; errors -> isError.
2. tests; rebuild per rules.

assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

T1  the whole point, end to end: A pinned, B init from pin@A + steps;
    A gains a definition and re-pins; rebase B -> steps stale;
    replay B -> a NEW step in B can use A's new definition.
    test [ml unit]: exactly that sequence; also rebase again ->
    "already up to date".
T2  stale pin blocks rebase with the resolve_spec error.
    test [ml unit]: mutate A without re-pinning; rebase B -> error
    "Pin \"A\" is stale".
T3  non-From_Theory origins are refused (forks, document/segment
    repls).
    test [ml unit]: fork C from B; rebase C -> error "does not
    support rebase".
T4  rebase does NOT replay (spec'd division of labor): after rebase,
    state -1 still shows the OLD final state until replay runs.
    test [ml unit]: assert stale marks present and state unchanged
    pre-replay.
T5  full chain.
    test [e2e]: the pin/rebase/replay workflow (extends
    plans/repl_pin T5).

